2.Which attribute makes an element's content editable by the use?
A)edit
B)writeable
C)contenteditable  //
D)useredit

✅ Correct Answer: C. contenteditable

Explanation (Simple)

The contenteditable attribute allows the user to edit the content of an HTML element directly in the browser.

Example:
<p contenteditable="true">
  Click here and edit this text.
</p>

The user can click inside the paragraph and type or modify the text.

Why not the others?
❌ A. edit → Not a valid HTML attribute.
❌ B. writeable → Not a valid HTML attribute.
✅ C. contenteditable → Correct! Makes an element editable.
❌ D. useredit → Not a valid HTML attribute.

✅ Correct Answer: C. contenteditable

3.Which attribute is used to group several options in a <select> dropdown?
A)group
B)optgroup  //answer
C)list
D)collection

✅ Correct Answer: B. optgroup

Explanation (Simple)

The <optgroup> element is used to group related <option> items inside a <select> dropdown.

Example:
<select>
  <optgroup label="Fruits">
    <option>Apple</option>
    <option>Banana</option>
  </optgroup>

  <optgroup label="Vegetables">
    <option>Carrot</option>
    <option>Potato</option>
  </optgroup>
</select>

This creates a dropdown with two groups:

Fruits
Vegetables
Why not the others?
❌ A. group → Not a valid HTML element/attribute.
✅ B. optgroup → Correct! Used to group options in a <select>.
❌ C. list → Used with <input> and <datalist>, not for grouping <option> elements.
❌ D. collection → Not a valid HTML element/attribute.

✅ Correct Answer: B. optgroup


4.Which attribute defines the HTTP method used when sending form-data?
A)action
B)route
C)method   /answer
D)type

✅ Correct Answer: C. method

Explanation (Simple)

The method attribute of the <form> tag specifies the HTTP method used to send the form data to the server.

The two most common values are:

GET → Sends data in the URL.
POST → Sends data in the request body.
Example:
<form action="/submit" method="post">
  <input type="text" name="username">
  <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form>
Why not the others?
❌ A. action → Specifies where the form data is sent.
❌ B. route → Not a valid HTML form attribute.
✅ C. method → Defines the HTTP method (GET or POST).
❌ D. type → Specifies the type of an input element, not the form submission method.

✅ Correct Answer: C. method


